00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.380
And how do you think
about diversity,

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.680
and how do you scale the
team and grow as you think

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.180
about diversity and inclusion.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.190
So it's been a really
fun and interesting ride.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:27.005
And it's great to be here,
happy Women's Day to everyone.

00:05:27.005 --> 00:05:29.380
YOLANDA MANGOLINI: I can see
we have an incredible wealth

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.065
of experience on this panel.

00:05:31.065 --> 00:05:33.190
But let's get right to the
question that's probably

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:34.870
on a lot of people's
minds when it comes

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.310
to diversity and inclusion.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.300
As women who are
leaders in technology,

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.216
do you think the environment
in Silicon Valley

00:05:40.216 --> 00:05:42.486
has gotten worse or better
over the past few years?

00:05:42.486 --> 00:05:44.110
And are you optimistic
about the future

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.210
for women in our industry?

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.790
Anyone can respond.

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:50.290
BRIDGET FREY: So
early in my career,

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:51.873
there were a lot of
situations where I

00:05:51.873 --> 00:05:53.740
was the only woman in the room.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:56.260
So for example, when I
was an intern at Motorola,

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:59.020
I worked on a floor
of 150 engineers.

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.930
And there were only two women,
me and the group assistant.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.450
So we had an entire bathroom
with a lounge and couch

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:05.170
all to ourselves.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:07.670
But other than that, there
were very few advantages

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:08.800
to that situation.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.260
And so I think it
has really changed.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.930
The numbers, I
think, are improving.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:17.200
At Redfin, we now have about
30% of our software engineers

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:18.130
are women.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.120
And I think even
more importantly,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.510
the number of women have
been able to communicate

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:23.939
with each other
and compare notes.

00:06:23.939 --> 00:06:25.480
So back when I was
at Motorola, there

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:27.400
was no Twitter to hear
other people's stories.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.620
There was no Google
Spreadsheets where you could

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.570
collect data and compare notes.

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:33.880
And so I'm just seeing a
lot of increasing advocacy

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.250
where women are coming
together and saying,

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:37.340
what's it like for you.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:38.620
Here's what it's like for me.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:41.110
And then use that data
to advocate for change.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:42.610
JEANETTE CALANDRA:
Yeah, it's funny.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.130
I think Silicon Valley gets
a pretty bad rap for this.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.470
And I would say being
out here for the last 10,

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:51.370
12 years is that I really
have seen a big shift.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:54.100
And that's whether
it's in PWC specific

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.970
or when we're out at our
clients and at the boards.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.110
So I remember a
similar experience

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.420
when I first came
out here in 2005.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:04.450
Very few females
or diverse partners

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:05.770
in our San Jose office.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.440
And I remember that
they had put together

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.030
some internal
marketing material that

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.196
was meant to be kind of
something beneficial, right?

00:07:12.196 --> 00:07:14.260
It was like highlighting
the women of San Jose.

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.426
And when you really looked
through it, first of all,

00:07:16.426 --> 00:07:19.450
the number of partners
versus other senior managers

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.014
or other managers was
very far and few between.

00:07:22.014 --> 00:07:24.180
And then in the bios, when
they kind of went through

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.920
and you kind of start looking
at the few female partners

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:30.370
that there were, one,
weren't too diverse, and two,

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:33.260
they were either unmarried
or they had this little thing

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.990
about my husband
stayed at home at work

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:36.670
to take care of the kids.

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.866
And so this wasn't
really inspiring

00:07:38.866 --> 00:07:43.780
as a career focused
a woman, who had

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.630
a partner at the time who was
very focused on his career

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:47.500
as well.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:53.140
And so looking kind of what has
happened in the last 10 years,

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:56.770
now we're really proud
to share that if you

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:59.410
look at our last couple of
incoming partner classes,

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:01.120
we're 30% women now.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.245
We're 43% minorities.

00:08:03.245 --> 00:08:04.370
We still have a ways to go.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:05.470
Because when you
kind of match that

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:07.945
up to the people who are
coming in to the pipeline, who

00:08:07.945 --> 00:08:09.400
are starting with
the firm, we're

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:12.940
much more diverse at the
in part of the funnel.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:14.530
But we're getting much better.

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:16.090
And then even on
the client side,

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:17.881
I mean, you go into
these meetings out here

00:08:17.881 --> 00:08:20.110
and you'd be the only
female in the room.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:22.780
And now I love it, I go
into some meetings and all

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:24.010
of a sudden, it's all women.

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:24.900
And you go, OK, wait.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.180
Who-- does anyone letting
the men in here, right?

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.730
And you're seeing those same
trends at the board as well.

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:32.564
So really promising, I think.

00:08:32.564 --> 00:08:34.480
SOWMYA SUBRAMANIAN: I
think mine is very, very

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:37.049
similar to Bridget's, actually.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.350
When I started, I
used to be-- it just

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:41.110
would be very common
to be the only woman

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:43.539
in the room as an engineer.

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:45.880
And one of the things
that I see has changed

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.090
a lot in the last 15
years in the industry

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:51.400
is there are so many more women.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:55.640
And just many more different
types of people, not just

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:56.140
women.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.360
From different backgrounds,
different countries,

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.320
different languages,
who are actually there

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.990
at the engineering level
when you look around.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.210
So I think that's been
a huge shift, which

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:08.980
is really nice to see.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:10.900
I must say, though,
I think there's still

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:13.810
a lot more we could be doing
at the leadership level.

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:15.880
It's great to hear
stats like yours.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.340
But I don't know
that that's the norm.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:22.030
Like in technology, for
instance, yes, some of us

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:22.530
are here.

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.330
We founded companies
and are CTOs.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:30.490
But I do feel that it's again
the next phase, where when you

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.280
get to that leadership
level, you many times

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.800
are the only woman
in the room again.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:40.270
And that's where I think there's
an opportunity to change.

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:42.760
The other good change
that has happened,

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:45.670
the shift that has happened,
is it seems much more

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:49.150
open and OK to talk about it.

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.680
Like we're having
a panel like this.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.900
Which I don't know
such a panel would

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:58.371
have happened at the next cloud
conference or Oracle OpenWorld.

00:09:58.371 --> 00:09:58.870
Right?

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:03.130
Talking about how do
we change culture.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:07.035
And I think that's
a huge step forward.

00:10:07.035 --> 00:10:08.410
CAROLINE TSAY:
Yeah, I completely

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:11.410
agree with Sowmya on
the point that there's

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:15.470
a lot of visibility into things,
or lots of research studies.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:17.890
It's a much more
open conversation.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:19.750
But I think too
many times now, I've

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:22.900
still seen that slide
that says here's

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:25.900
the number of this, this
type of demographic,

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:27.520
this type of background.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:32.440
And it's talked about, but not
much has been done about it.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:35.350
So you know, I think it's
drawing that line between what

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:38.230
are we seeing progressing and
changing in terms of diversity

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:41.560
of backgrounds, demographics,
skill sets, experiences,

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:42.970
thought.

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:46.360
This is something that's talked
quite a bit at the board level.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:49.270
I sit on two public
company boards.

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:51.520
Something we talk
about all the time.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.030
But ultimately, you know, it is
up to you, I think each of us,

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:59.560
to take some kind of action
to make that progress.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.080
AARTHI RAMAMURTHY: Speaking
from a startup's perspective,

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:05.710
I think for a larger
company, larger companies,

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:10.230
I think there's some
pressure to have a specific--

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:13.390
diversity is important,
inclusions is important.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:17.410
There are standards in place
to go meet specific criteria.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:19.480
In the case of startups,
there's no such thing.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.700
And I think there's
still a long way to go.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:26.980
I think a lot of it comes from
just having channels, access

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:32.020
to other founders, mentors,
availability of board members

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:33.130
who are women.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.380
Just opening up different
channels of communication,

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.930
I think there's a
long way to go there.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:40.120
Any kind of access, supply--

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:41.800
conferences like this are great.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.410
So I'm very optimistic
about how far we've come.

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:46.510
There's a long way to go.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:49.240
Counterpoint to what Sowmya
was saying was for me

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.024
it's the fact that
we're still here,

00:11:51.024 --> 00:11:52.690
and we're still talking
about diversity,

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:55.150
and we're still talking
about how much we

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:58.170
have to go do tells us
that we're still not there.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.250
And there's still
a long way to go.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:01.900
YOLANDA MANGOLINI: Absolutely.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:04.581
But it's nice that what we see
from optimism across the panel,

00:12:04.581 --> 00:12:05.080
right?

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:07.889
That's sort of good news.

00:12:07.889 --> 00:12:10.180
I spent the past seven years
trying to convince leaders

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:15.070
that having diverse teams can
improve bottom line results,

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.420
especially with regards
to creativity and design.

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:18.860
With mixed degrees of success.

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:22.780
Although I say by and
large, most leaders get it.

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:25.450
So I have a two part
question for any taker.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:27.370
Do you personally
think this is true,

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:31.240
that having heterogeneous
teams lead to better results?

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:32.800
And the second part
of the question,

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:36.470
what are your best arguments
for building diverse teams?

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:38.180
AARTHI RAMAMURTHY:
I can go here.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:40.580
For us, the way we
look at it is diversity

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:44.120
is important because you need
to reflect your customer base.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:45.860
We deal with the
world of gadgets.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:50.060
And traditionally, we've
been told, oh, gadgets,

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:52.220
consumer electronics,
it's all technophiles,

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:54.240
it's predominantly male.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.240
When we first
launched, we realized

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.330
that for specific verticals
like fitness trackers,

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:01.340
it's predominantly female.

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:02.330
That's the use case.

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:04.599
And so we had to go back
to different manufacturers

00:13:04.599 --> 00:13:06.140
and tell them, you
know, you're going

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:08.765
to have to design wearables that
are meant for a smaller wrist.

00:13:08.765 --> 00:13:11.570
And we had to come up
with these suggestions

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:14.600
on who our target base and
who our customers were.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:17.030
And I think having a
diverse team for us

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.700
to understand what was happening
and to reflect our customer

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:21.110
base was really helpful.

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:23.780
Which I don't think these
manufacturers and brands

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:25.189
necessarily did.

00:13:25.189 --> 00:13:27.230
Or else they could have
caught this a lot sooner.

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:29.960
So for me, diversity
is about reflecting

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:31.100
your customer base is.

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:36.770
And making sure that you have
a similar kind of percentage

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:40.190
or set of people that your
target audience would also look

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:41.000
like.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.500
That's kind of why I think
diversity is important.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:46.830
It's not meant to be
let's hit this number,

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:47.840
let's check a check box.

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:49.070
It's not meant for that.

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:51.500
It's meant to go solve
a specific problem.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:53.291
YOLANDA MANGOLINI: Are
there other examples

00:13:53.291 --> 00:13:55.875
besides to reflect on user base
that you all have encountered?

00:13:55.875 --> 00:13:57.707
JEANETTE CALANDRA: Yeah,
I mean, absolutely.

00:13:57.707 --> 00:13:58.690
I don't think it's--

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:00.120
kind of pulling from
that point, right--

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:01.830
I don't think it's about putting
a bunch of diverse people

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:04.290
in a room, and that's going
to give you the solution.

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:04.980
Right?

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:07.740
It's really creating
the environment

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:10.350
through differentiated
leadership and coaching

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:11.160
and mentoring.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:15.680
And being able to create that
safe environment for them

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.430
to be able to express
their point of view,

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:19.620
bring their perspectives.

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:23.585
And it was interesting, I
think early on in my career,

00:14:23.585 --> 00:14:26.580
I figured this all out
a little bit by default.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:30.150
We were in the middle of one
of the earlier financial crises

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.160
and regulatory reform.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:35.040
And we had a lot of our clients
needing big, big projects,

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.750
with Sarbanes-Oxley,
trying to figure out

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:38.542
what they're doing with
their organization.

00:14:38.542 --> 00:14:40.583
And somehow, I must have
stepped out for the day.

00:14:40.583 --> 00:14:42.730
But when I came back to
try to put together a team,

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:44.520
all of our top
staff were already

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:47.940
assigned to the other leading
clients we were serving.

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:50.820
So really had to kind of
find creative ways to bring

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:53.580
other people in from other
countries, from other offices,

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:55.170
to put together this team.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:58.620
And given the
backgrounds, I mean,

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:01.140
we hit every dimension of
diversity on this team.

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:03.810
And no one had the experience
of what we were doing.

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.060
So it was this interesting
experiment that said, look,

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:07.980
this is our team, we've
got to make it work.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.710
And really got to understand
each of those individuals

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:12.480
to kind of pull out
their strengths.

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:16.740
And over the two year period,
it was just amazing what we saw.

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:18.840
And we drove so much
value for the clients.

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:21.660
And I think when I realized
that we were completely

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:24.390
validated was when I
got the call from HR,

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:26.430
and we had gone through
the performance cycle,

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:27.846
and they said why
are you hoarding

00:15:27.846 --> 00:15:29.055
all the one rated staff.

00:15:29.055 --> 00:15:30.019
Right?

00:15:30.019 --> 00:15:31.560
And I said, that's
not how I started.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:32.060
Right?

00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:35.007
Nobody wanted pulling people
from different countries

00:15:35.007 --> 00:15:36.090
and different backgrounds.

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:38.130
I mean, people had changed
careers and just got

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:39.660
into professional services.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:41.850
But it took that team
working together.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:43.710
So it was kind of that
a-ha moment for me

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.134
to say, OK, you have to work
a little bit differently

00:15:46.134 --> 00:15:47.550
to bring out the
best in everyone.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.049
You're not hiding
their differences.

00:15:49.049 --> 00:15:51.580
You're really creating that
environment to promote them.

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:52.830
YOLANDA MANGOLINI: Absolutely.

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.870
BRIDGET FREY: And I really think
that high performing teams are

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:57.490
teams that are able to get a
lot of ideas onto the table,

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.880
and then figure out what
to do with those ideas.

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:02.724
There was some interesting
research into judges' panels.

00:16:02.724 --> 00:16:04.140
Where when you
have three judges--

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:06.870
if you have just one that has a
different perspectives of, say,

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.144
two conservative judges
and one liberal judge,

00:16:09.144 --> 00:16:10.560
the group will
make decisions that

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:11.787
are more towards the middle.

00:16:11.787 --> 00:16:13.620
And those two conservative
judges, you know,

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:15.870
they could outvote everyone
else that's on the panel.

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:18.180
Why is it that they end
up being more moderate?

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:20.370
And it seems to be that
it's because they considered

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:21.630
another point of view.

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:25.200
And so having a diverse team is
one way to sort of manufacture

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:27.690
that concept of having lots
of ideas to choose from.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:28.470
YOLANDA MANGOLINI: I love that.

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:29.026
I love that.

00:16:29.026 --> 00:16:33.630
One of the of the moment
topics is intersectionality.

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.020
We're not one dimensional.

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:36.659
I'm a black woman.

00:16:36.659 --> 00:16:37.200
I'm a mother.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.080
I'm from the east coast.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.700
I'm from a middle class family.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.980
And we all bring our
intersectionality

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:46.980
in our lives as leaders.

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:48.990
What can we learn from
the multiple angles

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.627
and perspectives that we bring
to diversity and inclusion?

00:16:51.627 --> 00:16:53.460
Again, any [INAUDIBLE],
any takers for that.

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:54.293
CAROLINE TSAY: Yeah.

00:16:54.293 --> 00:16:56.910
I think one thing that comes
to mind for me personally

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:59.400
is age as well.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:01.380
Sometimes it's a hard
topic to talk about

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:04.440
because you get into age
discrimination or things

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:06.390
like that.

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:08.180
But that's another
dimension in that

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:10.470
there's certain
experiences that come out

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.619
even at the board level of a lot
of public companies out there.

00:17:13.619 --> 00:17:17.490
You typically have average
ages of 66 plus there.

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:20.790
And you've got so much diversity
of thought and experiences

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:23.220
coming in from different
generations and backgrounds

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:24.660
as a result of that.

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:26.160
But I think one
thing to really keep

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:30.900
in mind too is the culture,
the values that people have.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:33.060
And it may be very
different for someone that

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:34.670
comes from another country.

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:35.640
Right?

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:38.550
At HP, we were very lucky
to hire a lot of folks

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:40.950
and have an h-1b
program that we put them

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.530
through right away, even
if they came in with a visa

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:46.740
and had several years into
getting through that visa.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:49.200
And I think what
that taught me was

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.020
that there are so many ways
and perspectives and things

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:53.580
that people value.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.850
Do they value relationships?

00:17:55.850 --> 00:18:01.110
Do they value technology
and hard core experiences

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:03.630
and thought around
implementation of a product?

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:05.000
Versus something else.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:06.790
And those things
start to come through

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:10.010
as you get that experience,
which I've been lucky to have.

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:11.970
And then you start
realizing that there's

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:14.700
so much more in the conversation
related to performance

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:17.250
management, objectives,
goals that people have,

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:19.060
that you want to keep in mind.

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:21.420
AARTHI RAMAMURTHY:
That makes sense.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:24.690
For us, when we look at it,
one is the customer base,

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:27.060
user base, reflecting on that.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.220
You're looking at the
immigrant population

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:32.970
making up for a huge
part of who we work with,

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:36.060
who our customers are,
who our neighbors are.

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:38.730
So it makes sense for us, for
our teams and our companies

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:40.290
to also reflect that.

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:45.287
And having this creative,
diverse mix of ideas is--

00:18:45.287 --> 00:18:47.370
some of these, I would
have never thought about it

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:48.745
because I didn't
go through that.

00:18:48.745 --> 00:18:51.760
I didn't study here, I didn't
have the same experience

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:52.960
as somebody else here.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.167
So for me, it's
amazing to go work

00:18:55.167 --> 00:18:56.500
with this diverse set of people.

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:59.350
It's very fulfilling,
and it's very gratifying

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:00.442
to be in that situation.

00:19:00.442 --> 00:19:01.900
BRIDGET FREY: We
have some examples

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:03.790
where intersectional
teams have really helped

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:05.560
us to build better products.

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:07.780
So we are working
on a feature where

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:09.790
if you're trying
to buy a home, you

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:12.140
could invite other people
to help you with the search,

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:13.480
so to connect with other people.

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:15.010
And in some of the
original mocks,

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:17.860
they showed something
like invite your spouse

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:19.300
to this group.

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:21.460
And because we had an
intersectional team that

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:24.567
had women, LGBTQ, that
team looked at those mocks

00:19:24.567 --> 00:19:26.650
and they said, hey, this
isn't inclusive language.

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:28.870
Instead, we should say
searching with someone else.

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:30.940
And I think at some point
in the product process,

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:32.939
maybe our customers would
have told us about it.

00:19:32.939 --> 00:19:36.790
But how amazing is it to find it
so early in the design process.

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:38.920
And just show that
intersectional teams

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:41.685
can create better results.

00:19:41.685 --> 00:19:43.060
SOWMYA SUBRAMANIAN:
Also, like, I

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:46.000
think a lot of this
discussion on diversity,

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.700
yes, it's about the team
and the team makeup.

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:52.930
It's also, I think, largely
about your growth strategy

00:19:52.930 --> 00:19:54.580
for your product.

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:57.130
So many times, what
we see, look around--

00:19:57.130 --> 00:19:59.200
and gadgets are a great example.

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.260
Including the Android Wear.

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:04.400
When it first came out, I
was like who designed this?

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:05.980
Now even if you
give it for free,

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:07.060
I'm not going to
wear this thing.

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:09.160
Even I'm from Google, and
it's not about our products.

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:10.160
It's an awesome product.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:13.570
But why wouldn't you
have designed something

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:15.640
a little prettier or nicer.

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:19.330
Or something like that
fits a small wrist.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:20.740
And it did change.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:22.840
And we had those
internal discussions

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:26.350
when we broadened the team,
and started pointing out

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:27.860
this kind of feedback.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:29.950
It's when we realized
actually we've completely

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:33.940
lost one kind of perspective
by focusing on maybe

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:36.430
the current dominant users.

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:38.710
So you go after the
current dominant users.

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:40.530
And you're a large--

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:43.130
most companies are
going in and saying,

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:46.337
I have these users who are
already engaged on my service.

00:20:46.337 --> 00:20:47.920
And I'm going to try
and see how can I

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:49.780
get them hooked even more.

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:52.300
And continue to keep
moving that further.

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:56.140
So a lot of our machine learning
algorithms or designs or user

00:20:56.140 --> 00:21:01.270
research ends up kind of fueling
that one dimensional thinking.

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:04.030
Which is an OK growth strategy.

00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:07.780
But then there's this whole pie
of people you've unconsciously

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:09.970
left out on the table.

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:13.870
And if you just pause to
say, I unintentionally left

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:16.820
these people out, the
segment of the population.

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:19.870
So why didn't I think about what
would it take to bring them in?

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:22.570
You actually might have a
much larger, a much more

00:21:22.570 --> 00:21:25.330
accelerated growth
plan than going

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:26.710
the route that you were going.

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:29.470
So for me, the whole discussion
about diversity-- yes,

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:33.080
it's about the funnel and
the team and all of that.

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:35.720
But at the end of the
day, it's not altruism.

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:36.220
Right?

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:37.000
It could be.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:40.180
But there's actual-- and we
were talking with us last night

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:43.570
at the tech maker's
mixer, right--

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:45.550
it's about making
money and growth.

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:47.477
It really does affect
your bottom line.

00:21:47.477 --> 00:21:49.310
BRIDGET FREY: I mean,
there's data out there

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:50.650
that demonstrates that.

00:21:50.650 --> 00:21:52.550
And this is such a
data driven community.

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:54.970
So many organizations
including Catulus,

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:56.762
they crunch the
numbers when you look

00:21:56.762 --> 00:21:58.720
at all these public
companies and the diversity

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:00.294
of their boards and
their leadership.

00:22:00.294 --> 00:22:02.710
And you look at the difference
between those companies who

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:05.631
are the least diverse
in the lower quartile

00:22:05.631 --> 00:22:06.880
and then those in the highest.

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:10.480
And the more diverse they are, I
mean whether you look at sales,

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:12.910
equity, return on
investment capital,

00:22:12.910 --> 00:22:14.620
the difference is astronomical.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:16.990
So you say, well, how are we
still in this conversation.

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:18.550
YOLANDA MANGOLINI:
Yes, absolutely.

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:21.800
So Jeanette, you've been
with PWC for 25 years,

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:22.690
as you mentioned.

00:22:22.690 --> 00:22:24.947
So you've probably advised
hundreds of companies.

00:22:24.947 --> 00:22:26.530
What's one of the
best examples you've

00:22:26.530 --> 00:22:29.252
seen of a client where
diversity inclusion makes

00:22:29.252 --> 00:22:31.210
a real impact on their
success and bottom line,

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:32.774
speaking of bottom line impact?

00:22:32.774 --> 00:22:33.940
What can we learn from them?

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:34.550
JEANETTE CALANDRA: Absolutely.

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:36.880
I can definitely share
a story where I think we

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:39.270
as PWC learned from when
I-- when you saw what

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:41.020
was going on at the
client, and we kind of

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:43.600
changed our strategy
and approach.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:47.590
So when I first came out
here, earlier in my career,

00:22:47.590 --> 00:22:49.160
I got put on a team.

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:53.380
I'm serving one of the largest
well-known technology companies

00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:54.420
out here in the valley.

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:58.660
And at that time, we maybe
had 20, 30 people service

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:00.100
serving this company.

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:04.480
And I was probably the most
diverse person of this team.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:09.310
And going out there in the
meetings with their leadership,

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:11.530
there was just a
complete mismatch

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:14.234
when you looked at the
diverse backgrounds that

00:23:14.234 --> 00:23:16.150
were coming to the table
to discuss a business

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:19.480
issue or a problem, versus what
we were bringing to the table.

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:22.240
And for I would say many
years, our relationship

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:24.370
was fairly stagnant.

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:27.260
And then I had the opportunity
to step into the lead partner

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:27.760
role.

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:29.650
And said, OK,
strategically, we're

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:30.400
going to do this all different.

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:32.566
I need to kind of bring in
not only the best talent,

00:23:32.566 --> 00:23:34.660
but think about
those diverse views

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:37.660
that are going to really
resonate with our customer.

00:23:37.660 --> 00:23:40.600
And so slowly
transitioned our team

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:44.560
to bring in a new set
of eyes, if you would.

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:47.020
And the results were magical.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:49.450
I mean, I love showing
the 10 year graph

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:51.179
of the success of this account.

00:23:51.179 --> 00:23:52.720
I mean for so long,
it was like this.

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:55.330
And all of a sudden when we
made that shift in the team

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:58.490
and worked differently
with our customer, I mean,

00:23:58.490 --> 00:23:59.840
it went straight up like that.

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:01.340
And you say, what happened here?

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:02.810
Like, we got more diverse.

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:04.561
I love this.

00:24:04.561 --> 00:24:05.060
All right.

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:08.502
So again, I kind of want to
tie in to one other point

00:24:08.502 --> 00:24:09.710
that we haven't touched upon.

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:11.168
I love-- I don't
know if any of you

00:24:11.168 --> 00:24:15.800
had read Pat Wadors' article
in the Harvard Business Review.

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:18.380
She is the CHRO of LinkedIn.

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:21.290
And so she goes one step further
beyond-- she calls it dibs.

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:24.860
You've got diversity,
inclusion, and then belonging.

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:28.970
And belonging being that feeling
of that part of that team being

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:31.430
valued is such a
critical part of creating

00:24:31.430 --> 00:24:35.060
that optimal performing team.

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:37.130
And so that has always
been a main tenet

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:40.100
kind of looking at our clients
who've done it really well.

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:41.480
They've created that belonging.

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:44.510
And how do we bring that into
how we're working with them.

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:45.760
YOLANDA MANGOLINI: Absolutely.

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:49.070
So Caroline, you've led teams
at large enterprises, HP

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:50.480
and Yahoo, for example.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:52.740
And at startups and
early stage companies.

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:55.120
Generally speaking,
do you think startups

00:24:55.120 --> 00:24:57.980
slash early stage companies or
more established tech companies

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:00.290
are better at putting
diversity at the center

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:01.370
of their business?

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:03.740
And can you contrast
your experiences

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:04.634
having been at both.

00:25:04.634 --> 00:25:06.050
CAROLINE TSAY:
Well, so it depends

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:07.216
on what you mean by startup.

00:25:07.216 --> 00:25:08.640
Startup could be
one person or it

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:10.480
could be thousands of people.

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:12.240
Startup is a big range.

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:15.470
I will say, though,
that in general, what I

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:18.710
got to see out of HP and Yahoo
is that the companies have

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:21.620
resources to invest
and spend time,

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:23.646
through the HR function
and organization,

00:25:23.646 --> 00:25:25.520
through teaching and
coaching, the leadership

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:28.340
and the management, to
invest in building up

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:31.637
pipelines of candidates
from other places.

00:25:31.637 --> 00:25:33.470
Whereas, you know, when
you're in a startup,

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:35.330
it's like go, go, go, go, go.

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:37.122
You just don't
think about anything

00:25:37.122 --> 00:25:39.080
except finding somebody
through your connection

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:42.050
as quickly as you can,
getting that introduction.

00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:44.890
And then very
quickly, you're kind

00:25:44.890 --> 00:25:47.990
of narrowing the pie
of opportunities or way

00:25:47.990 --> 00:25:49.130
you're thinking about this.

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:51.350
Also with a lot of larger
enterprises or bigger

00:25:51.350 --> 00:25:54.680
companies, they can
invest in h-1b visas,

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:56.840
I mentioned, or visas,
and funding that.

00:25:56.840 --> 00:25:59.780
Or relocating people from
other locations, which I think

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:02.540
is incredibly powerful.

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:04.880
Because you just get
so much diversity of

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:06.750
thought in the ways
that I mentioned,

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:09.740
and the values and
kind of the thinking.

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:11.810
So you know, I think about that.

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:14.170
But I highly encourage,
even for small companies--

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:17.060
I've seen too many kind of
smaller companies, startups,

00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:20.030
maybe a few people
who've been working

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:24.380
with each other for a long time,
who tend to talk or say things

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:27.140
that are like, oh my gosh,
wait till you go hire

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:29.720
that first woman into
the organization,

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:34.670
or first other person that is
very different from you guys.

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:36.650
I mean that very generically.

00:26:36.650 --> 00:26:40.250
We've got to think about
putting in place programs

00:26:40.250 --> 00:26:42.700
to be more thoughtful
about this.

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:44.960
So I do see quite a
bit of differences

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:46.940
when it comes to
larger, more established

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:49.700
companies, the resources
they have to bring to bear,

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:52.550
the way they're
thinking about this.

00:26:52.550 --> 00:26:55.850
Versus startups that generally
are more into this go,

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:57.200
go, go, mentality.

00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:59.158
YOLANDA MANGOLINI: It's
an interesting tension,

00:26:59.158 --> 00:27:01.370
because you would argue that
it's much-- particularly

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:02.480
from a representation
point of view--

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:04.938
it's much easier if you start
when you're smaller than when

00:27:04.938 --> 00:27:06.350
you're a 65,000 person company.

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:09.090
But it's a really great
point about the resources.

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:09.890
Bridget.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:12.759
So you got an early start
with computer programming.

00:27:12.759 --> 00:27:14.300
And you programmed
an Apple computer,

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:17.600
and that brings me back.

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:20.634
So it's something that many
girls don't get a chance to do.

00:27:20.634 --> 00:27:22.550
Do you think the tech
industry is doing enough

00:27:22.550 --> 00:27:24.140
to attract girls into our ranks?

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:26.169
And is it better or worse
now than 10 years ago?

00:27:26.169 --> 00:27:26.960
BRIDGET FREY: Yeah.

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:30.050
So I got my start with
computers when I was five.

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:33.320
My dad is an appliance
sales and repairmen.

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:36.380
And so when I was five,
he bought an Apple IIe.

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:38.450
And to him it was just
like another machine.

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:39.627
It was like a dishwasher.

00:27:39.627 --> 00:27:41.960
You get the manual, you figure
out how that thing works.

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:43.760
And that's what
the two of us did.

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:46.640
And so by the time I started
getting messages from society

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:48.650
that, you know, tech
wasn't for girls,

00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:50.460
I already loved computers.

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:53.160
So I was able to get
through that phase.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.940
But there are so many girls
for whom they're not getting

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:57.390
those really early experiences.

00:27:57.390 --> 00:27:59.304
And so I start to
shift to those messages

00:27:59.304 --> 00:28:00.470
that society is giving them.

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:02.990
And that's why I appreciate
work that Sowmya and her team

00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:04.940
and others are
doing, to look at how

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:06.350
the media is portraying things.

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:09.800
That it can be funny to talk
about the lonely, bedraggled

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:11.500
engineer working by themselves.

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:13.880
And you know, we're
all chuckling about it.

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:15.042
Or is it really funny?

00:28:15.042 --> 00:28:17.000
Or is it actually just
kind of a disturbing way

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:20.330
to perpetuate these stereotypes
that keep people out?

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:22.080
YOLANDA MANGOLINI:
So related, so

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.380
Sowmya, you lead a huge
part of YouTube's efforts

00:28:24.380 --> 00:28:25.838
to empower women
and girls, and you

00:28:25.838 --> 00:28:27.364
mentioned that in your intro.

00:28:27.364 --> 00:28:29.780
What can everyone here learn
about YouTube's efforts here?

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:31.905
And what lessons can folks
take away and bring back

00:28:31.905 --> 00:28:33.770
to their companies?

00:28:33.770 --> 00:28:37.160
SOWMYA SUBRAMANIAN: Yeah I think
your point that you brought up,

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:40.460
which is once you become
big, changing the numbers,

00:28:40.460 --> 00:28:44.180
even though you have so
many resources, it's harder,

00:28:44.180 --> 00:28:45.980
it's very, very real.

00:28:45.980 --> 00:28:48.150
I think there are
challenges on both sides,

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:51.050
whether you're a small or
you're resource constrained,

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:52.970
and you're just taking
what are you get.

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:53.470
Right?

00:28:53.470 --> 00:28:55.650
Not whatever, but
what quality bars.

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:57.300
Or you're big.

00:28:57.300 --> 00:29:00.750
And one of the things I
think we've done really

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:03.840
well in Google and
YouTube as a whole

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:08.770
is not be afraid to
question the status quo.

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:11.880
So we really do look at data.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:15.300
And we also look at the
processes that we have.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:18.510
And ask whether
things are working

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:21.150
because they were
working in the past,

00:29:21.150 --> 00:29:23.550
or is there a
better way to do it.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:25.710
So an example is--

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:28.740
and this is an example
outside of Google,

00:29:28.740 --> 00:29:32.670
but I use it within Google
to drive change with our HR

00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:35.180
and process organizations--

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:38.640
Maria Klawe, I don't know how
many people have heard of her.

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:42.990
She's the Dean of
Harvey Mudd College.

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:45.660
And she came from Yale, where
she was heading up the computer

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:47.010
science department.

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:50.370
And several years ago, I think
when she took on that role

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:52.980
in Harvey Mudd, she
decided that it's

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:56.319
going to be her mission in life
to change the number of girls

00:29:56.319 --> 00:29:58.110
entering into the
computer science program,

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:00.750
and women graduating
from her program.

00:30:00.750 --> 00:30:04.410
And she approached it
less about is there

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:08.520
a funnel problem, do I need to
encourage more girls and women.

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:11.640
She decoded it as,
she just observed

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:14.490
that a lot of the
entering class women

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:16.860
were not even signing up for
their introductory computer

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:18.150
science classes.

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:19.890
Because the way
it was structured

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:24.090
and the way it was
presented was alienating.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:27.810
Many of the incoming
men in the class

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:30.250
somehow had had
computer exposure

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:32.880
already by the time they
came to college compared

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:34.680
to the women, for some reason.

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:38.130
And the way the programming
assignments and everything is--

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:41.280
the way that the course
was being pitched

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:44.820
was set the bar so high that
people were just afraid,

00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:46.590
saying, this is not for me.

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:48.954
So she redid the
whole curriculum.

00:30:48.954 --> 00:30:51.120
And I think they don't call
them by numbers anymore.

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:54.300
They do like bronze,
gold, silver levels.

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:57.800
And she really made the
introductory levels fun,

00:30:57.800 --> 00:30:59.810
and a lot of team
activities, and much more

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:05.820
of a gentler introduction into
the field to build confidence.

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:09.330
And what she started seeing
is more women actually realize

00:31:09.330 --> 00:31:13.230
that not only do they enjoy it,
they're also really good at it.

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:16.950
And they should be considering
this more seriously.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:19.500
So did she change the
bar of her program?

00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:20.210
No, she did not.

00:31:20.210 --> 00:31:23.640
But she just restructured it
to make it more appealing.

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:25.860
And now, I think,
as of last year

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:29.025
they have a 50/50 graduating
class of men and women

00:31:29.025 --> 00:31:30.400
for the computer
science program.

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:32.310
Which is a huge
change to bring about

00:31:32.310 --> 00:31:34.660
in a few year time period.

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:37.680
So I think for
companies like Google,

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:40.410
because we have the
resources, the opportunity--

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:43.770
and we do use the opportunity,
actually, we've done this--

00:31:43.770 --> 00:31:46.710
is look at how your
promotion process works.

00:31:46.710 --> 00:31:49.320
Look at how performance
evaluations are done.

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:51.360
Look at how
interviewing is done.

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:53.730
And see, are there things
that we could be changing

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:56.050
to make it more inclusive.

00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:58.740
So one of the things Google
did several years ago

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.750
is the interview
panels, they make

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:04.260
sure there's at least
one woman on the panel.

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:07.530
They make sure it's a diverse
group of interviewers.

00:32:07.530 --> 00:32:10.200
Not just for the female
candidates, but all candidates.

00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:13.920
So you get a diverse
perspective on this person,

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:16.470
rather than this one
kind of, like, I'm just

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:19.596
projecting myself on everyone.

00:32:19.596 --> 00:32:20.970
So yeah, I think
there are things

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:22.060
we've done that are working.

00:32:22.060 --> 00:32:23.768
YOLANDA MANGOLINI:
They've very powerful.

00:32:23.768 --> 00:32:26.030
I agree, having
been in that space.

00:32:26.030 --> 00:32:28.590
Aarthi, so you were
quoted in Business Insider

00:32:28.590 --> 00:32:30.234
that your time at
Microsoft inspired

00:32:30.234 --> 00:32:31.650
you to build a
business that would

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:33.120
cater to millions of people.

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:34.770
And your time at
Netflix taught you

00:32:34.770 --> 00:32:36.840
how to create a
healthy team dynamic.

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:39.212
How did diversity play
into those experiences?

00:32:39.212 --> 00:32:40.920
And what are the most
important learnings

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:42.230
that you brought to Lumoid?

00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:43.230
AARTHI RAMAMURTHY: Yeah.

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:46.236
You know how-- it might not
apply to you on a day to day

00:32:46.236 --> 00:32:47.610
basis, but every
once in a while,

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:50.190
you'll have one of those
days where you'd be like,

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:53.520
if I did this on my own, I
would do things differently.

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:57.660
If only I could do this my
way, I would do it differently.

00:32:57.660 --> 00:32:58.920
Well, now I can.

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:01.680
And it's incredible
to have that feeling.

00:33:01.680 --> 00:33:03.614
And startups are not easy.

00:33:03.614 --> 00:33:05.280
I don't mean to say
that they're simple.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:06.113
They're really hard.

00:33:06.113 --> 00:33:07.920
This is the hardest
thing I've ever done.

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:11.690
But with respect to
diversity and inclusion,

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:15.510
there is so much freedom to
go do tests and experiments.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:19.080
And really prove out that
you're not doing this just

00:33:19.080 --> 00:33:20.020
for the sake of it.

00:33:20.020 --> 00:33:21.605
And it's actually profitable.

00:33:21.605 --> 00:33:22.980
You're leaving
money on the table

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:24.520
if you're not
considering diversity.

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:29.190
And being able to think about
it with a very data-centric,

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:31.410
result-centric approach
has been really great.

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:33.915
Even though, you know,
ours is a small company,

00:33:33.915 --> 00:33:35.280
we're about 40 people.

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:39.634
So it's nowhere the size
of Microsoft or Netflix.

00:33:39.634 --> 00:33:42.300
But we're able to think about it
in a much more independent way.

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:44.250
And that's been
incredible for us

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:46.770
as we grow and scale the
culture of the company.

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:50.550
Because in the future, when
we have directors and managers

00:33:50.550 --> 00:33:52.750
within the company, we want
them to think about this

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:57.309
as I'm not doing this just
to go fill out this form

00:33:57.309 --> 00:33:58.850
and make sure that
there's diversity.

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:01.330
But I'm doing this because
this is the right business

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:02.470
decision for the company.

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:05.350
This is how I want to
think about the future.

00:34:05.350 --> 00:34:09.130
And similar to Bridget, I
think, I started writing code

00:34:09.130 --> 00:34:10.844
when I was 12.

00:34:10.844 --> 00:34:12.260
Started getting
into Common-Lisp--

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:15.485
I don't even know if anyone
knows the language here.

00:34:15.485 --> 00:34:17.110
And started writing
my own interpreter.

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:19.719
And by the time people
started saying, oh, it's

00:34:19.719 --> 00:34:21.920
not meant for women,
it was too late.

00:34:21.920 --> 00:34:23.440
You already have me.

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:25.159
Too bad, can't do
anything about it.

00:34:25.159 --> 00:34:28.179
So it's about getting
the right set of people.

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:31.000
And for a startup, I
think you do whatever it

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:32.676
takes to find the right person.

00:34:32.676 --> 00:34:34.300
It's usually through
personal networks.

00:34:34.300 --> 00:34:38.409
And it turns out that I'm
a diverse person, just

00:34:38.409 --> 00:34:40.030
a background and
where I come from.

00:34:40.030 --> 00:34:42.710
And so my team is
extremely diverse.

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:44.770
We have people from
all over the world.

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:45.940
And it's great.

00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:47.199
It's working out really well.

00:34:47.199 --> 00:34:51.805
And we have arguments
on how things should go

00:34:51.805 --> 00:34:52.929
and how things should work.

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:57.176
But it's never in the context
of I don't really know,

00:34:57.176 --> 00:34:58.550
let's just do this
kind of thing.

00:34:58.550 --> 00:35:00.714
But it's more like
where I come from,

00:35:00.714 --> 00:35:02.380
this is how we've
done it kind of thing.

00:35:02.380 --> 00:35:04.296
And it's a really
nice place to be.

00:35:04.296 --> 00:35:05.920
There's a long way
to go for all of us.

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:10.744
But proving to yourself that
diversity works is the number

00:35:10.744 --> 00:35:12.160
one thing, it's
the simplest thing

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:14.620
that you can do instead
of just going with they

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:16.150
said we have to
have diverse people,

00:35:16.150 --> 00:35:18.159
so let's focus on diversity.

00:35:18.159 --> 00:35:20.200
You need to test it out
for yourself to make sure

00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:22.306
that you know that it works.

00:35:22.306 --> 00:35:24.430
YOLANDA MANGOLINI: So one
last question for you all

00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:25.971
before we open it
up to the audience.

00:35:25.971 --> 00:35:27.830
So start thinking
about your questions.

00:35:27.830 --> 00:35:30.040
Each of you have risen to
the top of your industry

00:35:30.040 --> 00:35:34.430
which is not an easy task
for a man, let alone a woman.

00:35:34.430 --> 00:35:38.050
When we know all the systemic
biases and structural biases

00:35:38.050 --> 00:35:39.080
that exist.

00:35:39.080 --> 00:35:41.440
So I'd love to hear, and
I'm sure the audience would

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:43.190
love to hear a little
bit from each of you

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:44.680
about your own journey.

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:47.890
What barriers have you had
to overcome, and specifically

00:35:47.890 --> 00:35:50.029
as a woman in
technology, to succeed.

00:35:50.029 --> 00:35:51.820
BRIDGET FREY: Well,
one thing that Jeanette

00:35:51.820 --> 00:35:53.920
touched on a little
is just how we make

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:55.600
tech inclusive for parents.

00:35:55.600 --> 00:35:56.920
So I have two boys.

00:35:56.920 --> 00:35:59.500
I think a number of our
panelists also have children.

00:35:59.500 --> 00:36:02.460
And how do you negotiate that
part of your career, I think,

00:36:02.460 --> 00:36:04.540
is something that tech
could really get better.

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:07.880
When I joined Redfin, I
already had two children.

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:11.480
And I felt lucky that I had
known the CEO for a long time,

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:12.200
for many years.

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:13.930
And he was willing
to sit down with me

00:36:13.930 --> 00:36:16.013
and have a really direct
conversation about what I

00:36:16.013 --> 00:36:18.100
needed, what types
of hours, about how

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:21.310
I would preserve that family
time in the evening that

00:36:21.310 --> 00:36:22.450
was so important to me.

00:36:22.450 --> 00:36:23.770
And we were able to
figure out something

00:36:23.770 --> 00:36:24.940
that worked for both of us.

00:36:24.940 --> 00:36:27.070
And it was so important
during those years.

00:36:27.070 --> 00:36:29.350
So for anyone who's
a manager out there

00:36:29.350 --> 00:36:31.450
or someone who's
working with people

00:36:31.450 --> 00:36:34.810
who are going to be parents
or have leaves coming up,

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:36.430
I would ask you to
focus more on what

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:39.040
it's going to be like for that
person when they come back.

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:40.790
There's so much focus
on when the person's

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.790
going to be out for a few
weeks or a few months.

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:43.840
But that's not the issue.

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:46.690
The issue is how are you going
to support that employee when

00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:49.210
they get back, and when their
kids' needs are changing

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:51.379
over time.

00:36:51.379 --> 00:36:53.670
JEANETTE CALANDRA: So when
I think about the barriers--

00:36:53.670 --> 00:36:55.830
and they started from
even before I embarked

00:36:55.830 --> 00:36:57.390
on my career--

00:36:57.390 --> 00:37:01.040
you wonder how sometimes you
got you got where you got to.

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:03.990
And kind of to share one
of the earliest stories,

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:07.500
so I was a math and numbers
geek when I was a teenager.

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:09.570
So very early on,
I had a passion

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:10.890
for what I wanted to do.

00:37:10.890 --> 00:37:14.040
And I remember the
time very vividly

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:16.650
that I was babysitting
for this local family.

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:21.330
And the dad of the family,
he owned his own CPA firm.

00:37:21.330 --> 00:37:22.830
So when I came to
this realization

00:37:22.830 --> 00:37:24.827
that this is the path
of accountancy I wanted

00:37:24.827 --> 00:37:26.910
to go down, I just remember
coming home one night,

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:28.368
he was driving me
back to my house.

00:37:28.368 --> 00:37:31.050
And I was so excited to
share, kind of I figured out--

00:37:31.050 --> 00:37:32.070
you know, everyone
asks you, what do

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:33.361
you do when you grow up, right.

00:37:33.361 --> 00:37:34.620
So I finally figured this out.

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:37.320
And so I broke the
news in the car.

00:37:37.320 --> 00:37:40.359
I mean, he literally stopped the
car, pulled over to the side.

00:37:40.359 --> 00:37:42.150
And looked at me and
he goes, why would you

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:44.070
do something stupid like that?

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.440
Women are not going to be
successful in accounting

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:47.710
or business.

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:50.807
And just like hung
there in the car.

00:37:50.807 --> 00:37:52.890
And so I was kind of one
of these pivotal moments.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:54.600
And you could either
have taken that

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:56.010
and gone down a different path.

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:58.560
Or it just created
this passion in me,

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:00.490
like I'm going to
prove him wrong.

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:02.280
Let me keep on going
down this journey.

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:04.740
And I think I had a
number of those times

00:38:04.740 --> 00:38:08.310
throughout my career where
there's a lot of support,

00:38:08.310 --> 00:38:10.972
but other times where you get
this reaction from someone,

00:38:10.972 --> 00:38:12.930
where you're really
surprised at that reaction.

00:38:12.930 --> 00:38:14.670
And I guess the
advice I would say

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:17.707
is you've got to look
past the statistics,

00:38:17.707 --> 00:38:18.540
and push through it.

00:38:18.540 --> 00:38:21.944
Because you got to be part of
the effort to make that change.

00:38:21.944 --> 00:38:24.360
SOWMYA SUBRAMANIAN: Yeah, mine
is very different actually.

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:26.310
So my mom is a math teacher.

00:38:26.310 --> 00:38:30.360
And I grew up always kind of
enjoying math and the numbers.

00:38:30.360 --> 00:38:33.900
And I never really defined
myself by my gender,

00:38:33.900 --> 00:38:35.940
that I can do this or not do it.

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:42.750
And I think the barriers
are more subtle in my world.

00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:46.530
Because I always had the support
of my family to be a computer

00:38:46.530 --> 00:38:47.820
science engineer.

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:50.040
I started out as
an engineer too.

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:53.310
Had been exposed to computers
at a very early age.

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:57.550
There was never really a
barrier because I was a woman

00:38:57.550 --> 00:39:00.000
until, I think, I started work.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:03.570
And even at work, it was less
about someone's telling you not

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:07.440
to do it because you're a
woman, to not just seeing people

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:09.810
around you who are like you.

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:14.800
So I still remember, I was
an intern at this company.

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:21.660
And one of these really
senior engineers, and he

00:39:21.660 --> 00:39:23.310
was very well respected
in the company,

00:39:23.310 --> 00:39:24.880
and he's a great friend now.

00:39:24.880 --> 00:39:27.520
But at that time, he happened
to run into me at lunch.

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:30.750
And he said, he was asking
me, oh, you're an intern.

00:39:30.750 --> 00:39:33.090
And he kept talking
to me, all along,

00:39:33.090 --> 00:39:36.490
assuming I was a marketing
intern for some reason.

00:39:36.490 --> 00:39:41.017
And then as we were walking
back, he saw me go to the floor

00:39:41.017 --> 00:39:42.600
where all the database
engineers were.

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:44.462
We were building the
database systems.

00:39:44.462 --> 00:39:46.170
And he was like, why
are you coming here?

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:47.045
Are you following me?

00:39:47.045 --> 00:39:49.440
I said no, I'm
going to my office.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:51.420
And he said, oh, you code.

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:55.470
And the surprise on his
face was kind of like--

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:56.850
It's many times,
you know, I went

00:39:56.850 --> 00:39:59.670
to undergrad at Mount
Holyoke College.

00:39:59.670 --> 00:40:01.440
And I was an international
student there.

00:40:01.440 --> 00:40:03.180
And the international
orientation,

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:06.075
they told us, many people
might come and tell you

00:40:06.075 --> 00:40:08.280
you speak really good English.

00:40:08.280 --> 00:40:11.880
And you should thank them,
and use it as an opportunity

00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:14.340
to correct the
ignorance, and say

00:40:14.340 --> 00:40:17.100
English was my first
language growing up.

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:19.260
Like why would I not
speak good English?

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:21.700
So I did the same
thing with this guy.

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:26.790
Just told him, ha, you know, I
am a computer science student

00:40:26.790 --> 00:40:28.950
like anyone else from the
University of Wisconsin

00:40:28.950 --> 00:40:30.090
Madison.

00:40:30.090 --> 00:40:31.870
Why shouldn't I code?

00:40:31.870 --> 00:40:34.170
And then he was like, oh, OK.

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:35.250
And he just walked away.

00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:36.030
He didn't know what to say.

00:40:36.030 --> 00:40:37.882
And then later he
came back and told me,

00:40:37.882 --> 00:40:39.840
I was looking at all the
code you were writing,

00:40:39.840 --> 00:40:42.460
you know, your CLs and
they're pretty good.

00:40:42.460 --> 00:40:43.480
They're really good.

00:40:43.480 --> 00:40:45.210
And that was a shift, right?

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:50.140
So I think in my case of
barriers-- and along the way,

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:52.560
even now sometimes you
are like the minority

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:56.010
voice in the group,
kind of shifting people.

00:40:56.010 --> 00:41:00.630
And knowing not to doubt
yourself or your confidence,

00:41:00.630 --> 00:41:03.720
but use it in a very
practical way as to say,

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:05.850
if you believe this
is the way to go,

00:41:05.850 --> 00:41:09.180
or if you believe you have the
right skills, just push for it,

00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:11.370
and make it happen.

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:14.070
I think that's kind
of my barriers story.

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:16.487
More than like an explicit
barrier that I had to overcome.

00:41:16.487 --> 00:41:17.944
YOLANDA MANGOLINI:
That's powerful.

00:41:17.944 --> 00:41:19.199
Caroline.

00:41:19.199 --> 00:41:21.240
CAROLINE TSAY: So since
we're talking about a few

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:22.710
growing up stories,
I was actually

00:41:22.710 --> 00:41:24.810
told that I had to do
electrical engineering

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:27.850
or that my tuition
wouldn't get paid for.

00:41:27.850 --> 00:41:30.060
So I tried to look at e.

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:32.340
And then I actually
thought, OK, well,

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:35.530
maybe if I go to CS, that won't
be too far of a departure.

00:41:35.530 --> 00:41:38.620
And I will still get
my tuition paid for.

00:41:38.620 --> 00:41:41.130
So I actually ended up
taking my first CS class.

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:43.784
I think what was so
powerful, so gratifying is

00:41:43.784 --> 00:41:46.200
that when you're coding, you
can see the immediate results

00:41:46.200 --> 00:41:47.340
of what you're doing.

00:41:47.340 --> 00:41:50.190
Versus maybe some hardware
initiatives or projects,

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:52.440
it takes a little bit
longer to get there.

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:55.080
So I ended up doing
CS in undergrad.

00:41:55.080 --> 00:41:56.890
I've always stayed
in tech in my career.

00:41:56.890 --> 00:41:58.770
And I think one
of the challenges

00:41:58.770 --> 00:42:00.660
that I've always had
to really think about

00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:03.570
as I've progressed in my
career is how much do you

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:08.160
decide when you want to fit
in, to be like everyone else,

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:13.850
so that it feels
OK to everybody.

00:42:13.850 --> 00:42:15.306
You don't stand out in some way.

00:42:15.306 --> 00:42:16.680
And I mean this
in a way that you

00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:18.888
dress, the way that you
communicate, the way that you

00:42:18.888 --> 00:42:22.410
talk, the way that you present
yourself, the way that you

00:42:22.410 --> 00:42:23.610
put on makeup or not.

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:25.680
I mean, seriously,
down to that level.

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:28.260
I've had to think about
that through my career.

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:30.330
I think at the end
of the day, though,

00:42:30.330 --> 00:42:33.690
when you can think about your
strengths, what you bring

00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:36.240
to the table, and you've
got confidence around

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:41.030
that, all of those things
don't matter so much anymore.

00:42:41.030 --> 00:42:43.050
But there is a
bit of a fine line

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:44.940
in thinking about
when you fit in,

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:47.820
when you might not, that
I've had to really go through

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:50.160
in my career.

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:53.190
AARTHI RAMAMURTHY: I feel myself
vehemently agreeing and nodding

00:42:53.190 --> 00:42:54.150
to everyone here.

00:42:54.150 --> 00:42:56.430
Because I feel like
this is a first-- like I

00:42:56.430 --> 00:42:57.690
do a few of these panels.

00:42:57.690 --> 00:42:59.940
This is the first time I
feel like, oh my god, they're

00:42:59.940 --> 00:43:03.000
speaking my story.

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:05.520
And what Sowmya said,
what Bridget said,

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:09.360
it's just like it's insane
that all of us go through this.

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:13.530
And you know, these are
still pretty serious issues.

00:43:13.530 --> 00:43:17.430
I mean, I graduated 10
years ago, multiple jobs,

00:43:17.430 --> 00:43:19.630
and you still see this
on a day to day basis.

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:23.130
And when I graduated, master's
in software engineering,

00:43:23.130 --> 00:43:25.950
I think my graduating
class had 60% women.

00:43:25.950 --> 00:43:29.670
And for me, honestly,
coming from India to here,

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:33.900
being in India, it didn't
feel like this odd thing.

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:35.940
It was like, yeah, that's
kind of what you do.

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:37.230
You get a job.

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:38.760
You write code,
great, good for you.

00:43:38.760 --> 00:43:40.170
And you just move on.

00:43:40.170 --> 00:43:42.720
But when I came here they
were like oh, you write code.

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:43.560
Are you sure?

00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:45.300
And I'm like, yes, I'm sure.

00:43:45.300 --> 00:43:47.190
What kind of a question is that.

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:51.440
How do I tell you if I'm
like 100% sure or 80% sure?

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:54.552
It's just this
ridiculous sense of what

00:43:54.552 --> 00:43:56.760
you have to think about,
how you have to communicate.

00:43:56.760 --> 00:43:58.176
You can't really
snap at them, you

00:43:58.176 --> 00:43:59.930
have to be patient with them.

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:02.760
And you're like, you
go through all of this.

00:44:02.760 --> 00:44:05.470
In your mind, you just run
this whole flowchart of stuff.

00:44:05.470 --> 00:44:06.990
And you're like, not worth it.

00:44:06.990 --> 00:44:09.179
You know what, just move on.

00:44:09.179 --> 00:44:11.220
And I feel like all of us
have been through that.

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:15.240
And everything from graduation
to being in a team where I

00:44:15.240 --> 00:44:16.620
think I was one of three women.

00:44:16.620 --> 00:44:19.710
And the weirdest were
these happy hours.

00:44:19.710 --> 00:44:21.480
And you're like, what do you do?

00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:24.390
Like especially if one or
two women don't show up

00:44:24.390 --> 00:44:25.640
and you're the odd person out.

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:29.730
You're just like looking at
each other like am I being here,

00:44:29.730 --> 00:44:31.530
like, this is odd
for you people?

00:44:31.530 --> 00:44:33.266
Or is this the other way around?

00:44:33.266 --> 00:44:33.890
What do you do?

00:44:33.890 --> 00:44:36.540
So you have all these
like semi funny stories.

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:39.670
And semi oh my god,
this is really sad.

00:44:39.670 --> 00:44:43.940
And I get it, this
is kind of how it is.

00:44:43.940 --> 00:44:45.964
There's so few women
in computer science

00:44:45.964 --> 00:44:48.630
and in engineering and tech, and
we're working on changing that.

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:51.300
But today, right now, it's 6:00
p.m., and this is where we are,

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:52.508
and this is what we're doing.

00:44:52.508 --> 00:44:55.356
And I think you go
through that from there

00:44:55.356 --> 00:44:56.730
to building your
own startup, you

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:58.479
see a totally different
set of challenges.

00:44:58.479 --> 00:44:59.880
Which is fundraising.

00:44:59.880 --> 00:45:04.170
Most investors don't
really see female CEOs.

00:45:04.170 --> 00:45:06.600
And when you go into
pitch, they're like, oh,

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:07.630
so where's the founder.

00:45:07.630 --> 00:45:09.546
And you're like, well,
you're speaking to her.

00:45:09.546 --> 00:45:12.279
I'm sorry but it's
me, you just have me.

00:45:12.279 --> 00:45:14.070
And they're like, OK,
so where's the coding

00:45:14.070 --> 00:45:15.390
guy, like, the engineering guy.

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:18.240
And you're like uh, me, sorry.

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:20.070
And you're constantly
apologizing.

00:45:20.070 --> 00:45:22.160
And you're like, why
am I apologizing?

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:24.420
I'm just as good as
like other people here.

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:26.560
But you go through a
lot of this in your--

00:45:26.560 --> 00:45:29.160
it's come to a point where
I'm not cynical or jaded.

00:45:29.160 --> 00:45:30.810
But you just accept
that there is

00:45:30.810 --> 00:45:34.260
going to be this level of
people always not really

00:45:34.260 --> 00:45:35.700
knowing what you really do.

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:39.090
So now my job is to clearly
go out and establish

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:41.610
and communicate what I
do, and where I come from,

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:45.120
and this is how it is, and
this is the reality of what we

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:46.470
are doing, this is our startup.

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:50.230
And we get these assumptions
made all the time.

00:45:50.230 --> 00:45:51.300
And it is a challenge.

00:45:51.300 --> 00:45:52.260
It's not easy.

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:54.480
But it's getting
a little better.

00:45:54.480 --> 00:45:57.764
I mean, the more we do
stuff like this, the more--

00:45:57.764 --> 00:46:00.180
you know, you pulled out this
piece from Business Insider.

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:02.310
And I was like, hey,
somebody has read that.

00:46:02.310 --> 00:46:02.940
It's great.

00:46:02.940 --> 00:46:04.567
So it's changing.

00:46:04.567 --> 00:46:05.400
Things are changing.

00:46:05.400 --> 00:46:06.566
And it's all getting better.

00:46:06.566 --> 00:46:09.122
But some of these, you're
going to hear for a while.

00:46:09.122 --> 00:46:10.830
You're going to hear
these stories, which

00:46:10.830 --> 00:46:12.120
are like half amusing.

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:16.410
And there is-- I will tell you,
there are groups of women who

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:19.260
will sit around and talk
about this and be like,

00:46:19.260 --> 00:46:21.646
do you remember that
time when this happened,

00:46:21.646 --> 00:46:22.770
and we just laugh about it.

00:46:22.770 --> 00:46:24.330
But it's also kind of sad.

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:26.405
And we'll commiserate.

00:46:26.405 --> 00:46:28.530
And it's nice to have a
group of women who do that.

00:46:28.530 --> 00:46:32.730
And it's nice to see men
here, who are a part of this.

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:34.560
And you're not ignoring
this whole thing.

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:36.660
And these are challenges.

00:46:36.660 --> 00:46:38.340
And we will get
through all of them.

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:42.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

